# System Design — Complete Learning Roadmap (Beginner to Advanced)

## How to use this list
- Go topic by topic, in order
- Don't skip ahead — each group builds on the previous
- For each topic: read it, ask for notes, ask for diagrams, test yourself

===========================================================
## PHASE 1: Networking Foundations
(You need this before anything else)
===========================================================

1.  Client-Server Model          ← you already did this
2.  IP Addresses & Ports
3.  DNS (Domain Name System)
4.  HTTP & HTTPS
5.  TCP vs UDP
6.  REST APIs
7.  WebSockets

===========================================================
## PHASE 2: How the Web Actually Works
(What happens behind the scenes of every request)
===========================================================

8.  What happens when you type a URL and hit Enter
        (this topic ties Phase 1 together — very important)
9.  Latency vs Throughput
10. Bandwidth
11. Proxies & Reverse Proxies

===========================================================
## PHASE 3: Scalability
(You already did the intro — now go deep)
===========================================================

12. Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling  ← you already did this
13. Load Balancers
14. Caching (what it is, why it matters)
15. Cache strategies (Cache-aside, Write-through, Write-back)
16. CDN (Content Delivery Network)

===========================================================
## PHASE 4: Data Storage
(Databases are where most systems live or die)
===========================================================

17. Relational Databases (SQL) — how they work
18. Non-relational Databases (NoSQL) — types and when to use
19. SQL vs NoSQL — how to choose
20. Indexes — what they are and why they matter
21. Database Replication (Primary-Replica)
22. Database Sharding
23. CAP Theorem

===========================================================
## PHASE 5: Reliability & Availability
(Making systems that don't go down)
===========================================================

24. Availability & Uptime (the "nines" — 99.9%, 99.99%)
25. Fault Tolerance & Redundancy
26. Single Points of Failure
27. Rate Limiting
28. Consistent Hashing

===========================================================
## PHASE 6: Advanced Components
(The building blocks of large-scale systems)
===========================================================

29. Message Queues (Kafka, RabbitMQ — what and why)
30. Pub/Sub Pattern
31. Blob Storage (S3 — storing images, videos, files)
32. Search Systems (Elasticsearch — how search works at scale)
33. API Gateway

===========================================================
## PHASE 7: Putting It All Together
(System design thinking and patterns)
===========================================================

34. Microservices vs Monolith
35. Event-Driven Architecture
36. Back-of-the-envelope estimation
        (estimating scale: users, storage, requests per second)
37. How to approach a system design interview / problem

===========================================================
## PHASE 8: Practice — Design Real Systems
(Apply everything you learned)
===========================================================

38. Design a URL Shortener (like bit.ly)        ← start here
39. Design a Rate Limiter
40. Design a Key-Value Store
41. Design a Pastebin
42. Design Twitter / X feed
43. Design YouTube
44. Design WhatsApp / chat system
45. Design Instagram
46. Design Uber / ride-sharing
47. Design Google Drive / Dropbox
48. Design a Web Crawler
49. Design a Notification System
50. Design a Search Autocomplete

===========================================================
## QUICK REFERENCE — what each phase unlocks
===========================================================

Phase 1 → you understand HOW computers talk
Phase 2 → you understand WHAT happens in a real request
Phase 3 → you understand HOW to handle more users
Phase 4 → you understand HOW to store and retrieve data at scale
Phase 5 → you understand HOW to build systems that don't fail
Phase 6 → you understand the COMPONENTS used in large systems
Phase 7 → you can THINK like a system designer
Phase 8 → you can DESIGN real systems from scratch
